# NilClass#inspect

inspect() -> String

Returns "nil"

```ruby
p nil  #=> nil
```

# NilClass#nil?

nil?() -> bool

Returns true

**The way to test for nil.**
```ruby
return if v.nil?
```

# NilClass#&

&(untyped other) -> bool

Always returns false

```ruby
nil & true  #=> false
```

# NilClass#===

===(untyped other) -> bool

Equality as `case` uses it

```ruby
case v
when nil then missing
end
```

# NilClass#!

!() -> bool

Return logical NOT

nil is false, so this is always true.
```ruby
!nil  #=> true
```

# NilClass#|

|(untyped other) -> bool

Returns logical value of argument

```ruby
nil | true  #=> true
```

# NilClass#^

^(untyped other) -> bool

Returns logical value of argument

```ruby
nil ^ true  #=> true
```

# NilClass#to_f

to_f() -> Float

Returns 0.0

```ruby
nil.to_f  #=> 0.0
```

# NilClass#to_h

to_h() -> Hash

Returns empty hash

```ruby
nil.to_h  #=> {}
```

# NilClass#to_i

to_i() -> Integer

Returns 0

**It becomes 0**, so a value that failed to read can still be added.
```ruby
nil.to_i  #=> 0
```

# NilClass#to_s

to_s() -> String

Returns empty string

**An empty string**, not "nil".
```ruby
nil.to_s  #=> ""
```
# NilClass#inspect

inspect() -> String

"nil" „Å®„ÅÑ„ÅÜÊñáÂ≠óÂàó„ÇíËøî„Åô

